\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The attractor model of the number line (parameters fitted at 160~ms).} \textbf  {a}, Energy landscape over the internal evidence axis $x$: each integer is a well whose width grows with magnitude (the orange curve is the declining number frequency that motivates this widening), so small numbers sit in deep, isolated basins that clean up to an exact value. The dotted line marks the continuous width-based proxy $n_{\mathrm  {width}}$, where $\sigma _n = 1$; the topological analysis in Fig.~\ref {fig:comparison}e directly determines where consecutive integer basins disappear. \textbf  {b}, Expected readout entropy $E[H]$ per presented numerosity, before (grey) and after (black) clean-up, with the attractor's expected convergence time $E[T]$ (blue, right axis); insets show example post-clean-up posteriors. Clean-up removes the residual uncertainty of the small-$n$ readout at the cost of a few settling steps; near the boundary the settling time grows and the residual entropy rises. \textbf  {c}, The resulting response time, $\mathrm  {RT} = t_0 + \tau \,E[T] + \kappa \,E[H]$, decomposed into its basal, convergence-time, and readout-uncertainty components.}}{3}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:conceptual}{{1}{3}{\textbf {The attractor model of the number line (parameters fitted at 160~ms).} \textbf {a}, Energy landscape over the internal evidence axis $x$: each integer is a well whose width grows with magnitude (the orange curve is the declining number frequency that motivates this widening), so small numbers sit in deep, isolated basins that clean up to an exact value. The dotted line marks the continuous width-based proxy $n_{\mathrm {width}}$, where $\sigma _n = 1$; the topological analysis in Fig.~\ref {fig:comparison}e directly determines where consecutive integer basins disappear. \textbf {b}, Expected readout entropy $E[H]$ per presented numerosity, before (grey) and after (black) clean-up, with the attractor's expected convergence time $E[T]$ (blue, right axis); insets show example post-clean-up posteriors. Clean-up removes the residual uncertainty of the small-$n$ readout at the cost of a few settling steps; near the boundary the settling time grows and the residual entropy rises. \textbf {c}, The resulting response time, $\mathrm {RT} = t_0 + \tau \,E[T] + \kappa \,E[H]$, decomposed into its basal, convergence-time, and readout-uncertainty components}{figure.caption.4}{}}
