\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Model comparison across exposure durations, response time, and the predicted resolution indices.} The model comparison is shown two ways: the primary joint fit, in which a single landscape is shared across all five durations, and the secondary separate fits per duration. \textbf  {a}, Accuracy channel, joint fit: the power-law attractor and static readout share their landscape parameters ($\sigma _c$, $p$), while the constant-width controls fix $p=0$ and share only $\sigma _c$. Sensory parameters and lapse remain free per duration. Weber/ANS remains fully duration-specific. The bounded-optimal model of~\citet  {cheyette_unified_2020} (``Normative (C\&P)'') shares $B$ and $R$ but uses a duration-specific lapse, matching the lapse flexibility of the other models. Cells are $\Delta $AIC relative to the best model (darker is better). \textbf  {b}, Accuracy channel, separate fits per duration, over the ${\sim }53{,}000$ individual responses. The power-law wells ($\sigma _n = \sigma _c\,n^{p}$, Eq.~\eqref  {eq:width}) and constant-width controls ($\sigma _n=\sigma _c$) each appear with clean-up and as a matched static readout. For C\&P, both the information budget and lapse are fitted per duration. Clean-up wins at the briefest exposures, while the static readout catches up at 320--640~ms. \textbf  {c}, Response-time channel, joint fit: one RT mapping shared across durations. \textbf  {d}, Response-time channel, separate fits on the winning landscape. In both, the link combining the attractor's convergence time and the residual readout entropy is best; neither term alone is sufficient, and their balance shifts across exposures. \textbf  {e}, The fitted width law $\sigma _n$: the single shared landscape of the primary joint fit (black dashed) and the secondary per-duration fits (colours). Circles mark the continuous width-based index $n_{\mathrm  {width}}$, defined by $\sigma _n=1$. Squares mark $n_{\mathrm  {topo}}$, the last consecutive integer retaining its own local minimum separated by an energy barrier. The joint fit gives $n_{\mathrm  {width}} \approx 8.3$ and $n_{\mathrm  {topo}}=6$; the separate fits give $n_{\mathrm  {width}}\approx 3.5$--$9.8$ and $n_{\mathrm  {topo}}=2$--$7$.}}{5}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:comparison}{{2}{5}{\textbf {Model comparison across exposure durations, response time, and the predicted resolution indices.} The model comparison is shown two ways: the primary joint fit, in which a single landscape is shared across all five durations, and the secondary separate fits per duration. \textbf {a}, Accuracy channel, joint fit: the power-law attractor and static readout share their landscape parameters ($\sigma _c$, $p$), while the constant-width controls fix $p=0$ and share only $\sigma _c$. Sensory parameters and lapse remain free per duration. Weber/ANS remains fully duration-specific. The bounded-optimal model of~\citet {cheyette_unified_2020} (``Normative (C\&P)'') shares $B$ and $R$ but uses a duration-specific lapse, matching the lapse flexibility of the other models. Cells are $\Delta $AIC relative to the best model (darker is better). \textbf {b}, Accuracy channel, separate fits per duration, over the ${\sim }53{,}000$ individual responses. The power-law wells ($\sigma _n = \sigma _c\,n^{p}$, Eq.~\eqref {eq:width}) and constant-width controls ($\sigma _n=\sigma _c$) each appear with clean-up and as a matched static readout. For C\&P, both the information budget and lapse are fitted per duration. Clean-up wins at the briefest exposures, while the static readout catches up at 320--640~ms. \textbf {c}, Response-time channel, joint fit: one RT mapping shared across durations. \textbf {d}, Response-time channel, separate fits on the winning landscape. In both, the link combining the attractor's convergence time and the residual readout entropy is best; neither term alone is sufficient, and their balance shifts across exposures. \textbf {e}, The fitted width law $\sigma _n$: the single shared landscape of the primary joint fit (black dashed) and the secondary per-duration fits (colours). Circles mark the continuous width-based index $n_{\mathrm {width}}$, defined by $\sigma _n=1$. Squares mark $n_{\mathrm {topo}}$, the last consecutive integer retaining its own local minimum separated by an energy barrier. The joint fit gives $n_{\mathrm {width}} \approx 8.3$ and $n_{\mathrm {topo}}=6$; the separate fits give $n_{\mathrm {width}}\approx 3.5$--$9.8$ and $n_{\mathrm {topo}}=2$--$7$}{figure.caption.6}{}}
